Contact dermatitis (plaque)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Erythematous plaques corresponding to exposure, Intense pruritus/burning, Geometric or linear configurations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-014
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe weeping rash on the abdomen causing extreme pruritus and suspected secondary infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly worsening, intensely itchy, and oozing rash localized to the abdomen that has failed to improve with over-the-counter creams. The patient was highly distressed and unable to sleep. Urgent assessment was necessary to evaluate for secondary impetiginization, confirm the etiology of the persistent dermatitis, and provide immediate potent anti-inflammatory relief to break the severe itch cycle before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has this bright red, weeping rash that itches so much it's driving me crazy. The creams the pharmacist gave me did nothing."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an intensely pruritic and burning skin eruption on the lower abdomen that began 4 days ago. The lesion is a well-demarcated erythematous plaque corresponding exactly to the site where the metal buckle of a new belt rests against the skin. The patient initially noted xerosis and slight scaling, but continuous scratching has led to severe oozing, crusting, and superficial fissuring. The patient has been applying an over-the-counter antiseptic cream, which appears to have exacerbated the irritation. There is a complete lack of systemic symptoms. The relentless pruritus and weeping prompted the urgent request for assessment.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation due to intense pruritus.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 86 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, frequently lifting shirt to scratch at the abdomen.
Physical Exam: On the infraumbilical abdomen, there is a 6x4 cm sharply demarcated, rectangular erythematous plaque. The shape and location correspond exactly to a belt buckle exposure. The plaque exhibits significant superficial excoriation, serous oozing, and mild yellowish crusting. There is no spreading cellulitic erythema, deep induration, warmth, or purulent (foul-smelling) discharge. No regional lymphadenopathy is present. No vesicles or bullae are currently intact, but evidence of ruptured microvesicles is apparent in the intensely inflamed areas.
Environmental Observations: The offending metal belt is visible on a nearby chair.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient applied OTC Savlon cream; no active prescriptions.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Allergic Contact Dermatitis (Nickel/Metal induced) with secondary excoriation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tinea Corporis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly rectangular, geometric shape corresponding to the metal exposure, the intense weeping nature, and the lack of a classic annular scaly border with central clearing.

Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The primary intense pruritus, the geometric shape, and the presence of serous weeping rather than classic heavy honey-colored crusts.

Atopic Dermatitis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sharply localized nature of the plaque corresponding entirely to an external object, without involvement of typical flexural areas.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of allergic contact dermatitis secondary to metal exposure (likely nickel), complicated by severe excoriation and serous weeping. Triage appropriately identified the severe intractable itch and oozing as requiring urgent evaluation to rule out secondary bacterial infection and provide immediate relief. Clinical examination confirms the geometric nature of the contact dermatitis without definitive signs of overt bacterial superinfection. Immediate out-of-hours intervention was required to prescribe potent topical corticosteroids to suppress the acute inflammatory reaction and provide a clear directive to avoid the offending allergen, thereby preventing further tissue breakdown and an inevitable emergency department visit for intractable pruritus.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to immediately cease the OTC antiseptic cream, which may be acting as an additional irritant. Prescribed topical Mometasone furoate 0.1% ointment to be applied once daily to the affected area. Recommended a daily non-sedating antihistamine (Cetirizine 10mg) for symptomatic itch control.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly avoiding wearing belts with metal buckles that touch your skin, you will completely remove the trigger causing this reaction, allowing your skin to finally heal. By applying a thin layer of the prescribed steroid ointment only once a day and avoiding scratching, you will rapidly shut down the intense inflammation and stop the weeping within 48 hours.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to ensure complete resolution and discuss long-term allergen avoidance strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness begins spreading rapidly beyond the initial square shape, if the clear fluid turns into thick yellow pus, or if you develop a fever, you must seek immediate medical attention or go to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The weeping is part of the severe allergic reaction, but broken skin always carries a small risk of bacterial infection.
Clinical Course: The intense itching and oozing will stop within 1-2 days of using the steroid ointment and removing the metal contact.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the area for signs of infection such as spreading heat, swelling, or foul-smelling discharge.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have developed a severe allergy to the metal (likely nickel) in your belt buckle. The immune system reacted violently, causing the skin to blister and weep. The steroid cream will forcefully calm this immune reaction.
Actionable Steps: Do not use any more over-the-counter antiseptic creams. If you must wear a belt, ensure your shirt is tucked in to provide a thick physical barrier between the metal and your skin.

